  "final_text": "All four steps are done. I read the rule back after the edit, and it shows enabled.\n\n1. **Data view:** `Kibana Sample Data Logs` (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has fields such as `timestamp`, `response`, `bytes`, `clientip`, `host`, `extension` and `message`. Its time field is `timestamp`.\n2. **Dashboard:** `S4-df1be315 Web logs` has two panels. One is a line chart of request count over time. The other is a table of request count by `response.keyword`, showing up to 20 codes. I used a table because the xy chart schema rejects a terms bucket on the x-axis.\n3. **Rule:** `S4-df1be315 5xx errors` is an Elasticsearch query rule on the data view. It runs every 1m, looks back 5m, and fires when the count is above 10. The filter is `response.keyword >= \"500\" and response.keyword <= \"599\"`. It compares the codes as strings, which covers 500\u2013599 because every code has three digits. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards. I didn't check that it actually fires.\n4. **Edit:** the response-code panel is now titled `Responses by code`. The other panel is unchanged.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
